From 011a0ae2b60248670377606074cb14593d222b4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sambukuper <sambukuper@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 01:44:39 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Publish the world to anyone: e4mc ported

e4mc opens a LAN server to anyone, anywhere, anytime; Java mods do not
run on Horizon, so what the mod does arrives natively. The host connects
out to e4mc's public quiclime relay over QUIC, the relay assigns the
world a public domain, and every guest who joins the domain becomes one
bidirectional stream on the session, bridged byte for byte to the
integrated server's LAN port - the same shape as the Steam share and the
invite code, with a third transport in front of the same LAN port. The
address creates itself: no Steam, no port forwarding, no invitation, and
guests join from any client, vanilla's included.

crates/horizon-e4mc: the broker call, the control channel (varint-framed
JSON like the Java mod's codec), the guest bridges and the session
lifecycle on a private tokio runtime. The endpoint outlives the session
on purpose: dropping it kills connections silently, and the close frame
is what tells the relay to end its guests. Guest counters ride an RAII
guard, so a cancelled bridge still reports itself gone.

The Play with Friends screen gains the Anyone (e4mc) section: Publish to
Anyone, the green address with a copy button, Stop Publishing. The three
shares hand the LAN port over to each other instead of closing it under
the others' guests; the screen says the address is public before the
button does anything.

Tests: the codec and facade unit-tested; one --ignored test runs the
whole chain live against e4mc's public broker and relay - domain
assigned, a guest joining with a real Minecraft handshake, bytes both
ways, stop dropping the guest. It passed.

Co-Authored-By: ZCode (GLM-5.3-Flash) <noreply@z.ai>
